zed  and it's dragged on for a while there  are a couple reasons for that the first  is that as the investigation proceeded  and it was a pretty big investigation  all told more bugs were discovered we  talked about the static view but there's  also others like one that lets you  literally fly around the map like you  have no clip enabled and then another  where you're sort of locked to a third  person view another issue is that esic  frankly isn't loaded down with resources  they had to review nearly 100 000 demos  for this investigation and that's about  15 terabytes of data from what they've  said it's a lot okay so in august 2021  nearly a year after that initial  statement from esic they said that more  cases had surfaced but also that  punishments were a little difficult to  figure out since a lot of the cases  flagged were very short durations of bug  use sometimes just seconds now before  you respond with a witty retort i want  to lay out the situation in some of  these cases there was a bug that a coach  got for a round or less and they will  say at least that they didn't  communicate that information to their  team so what is an appropriate  punishment for that scenario what should  esic do sure they should have reported  it we all agree that was the correct  thing to do but having the bug for a  round or less  can you punish that in the same way as  this this clip is from e6 investigation  and features then heroic coach  peacemaker flying around the map scoping  out the cts peacemaker is now the coach  for imperial but he was one of three  coaches punished in e6 may 5th  announcement before the antwerp major  the other two were halley of team spirit  and zach of 9z team all three of these  teams are now competing at the antwerp  major without their coach because of the  timing of this announcement but how did  we get to this point well reports in  recent weeks from several media outlets  including decerto and dust2 claimed that  not only had the investigation ballooned  in size but also that some coaches who  had qualified for antwerp were in e6  sites in some way this matter had to be  dealt with before the major so  peacemaker hallie and zach were  suspended provisionally and that may 5th  announcement says there's more to come  they also say that there's a fourth  coach at the major that is also involved  in the investigation but that their  offenses are nowhere near as serious as  the three who were suspended  provisionally so that brings us back to  punishment now e6 view is that it  doesn't have to prove that the coach  gave the info to the players because  they're exploiting a bug that has the  potential to affect competitive  integrity that in itself is an offense  it doesn't have to prove that players  knew that the coach was using the bug  now e6 may 5th announcement says that  the investigation is being finalized and  also that more punishments are  forthcoming soon they also break down  the bugs clearly into three separate  categories firstly there's the original  spectator bug which they call the static  spectator bug which allows a coach to  set up a single pov somewhere on the map  where they can then rotate the camera so  these cases are being handled using the  initial punishment guidelines that esic  announced alongside the investigation  with things like confessions cooperation  and whatnot earning some reduced  sentences secondly we have the free roam  bug now if you remember that peacemaker  clip we just showed that's an example of  that you can essentially fly around the  map fly through surfaces  follow players instead of having a fixed  pov this is clearly more damaging to  competitive integrity than a static pov  and e6 says it's going to be punished  accordingly e6 says three people  exploited this free roam buck but i want  to be very clear it's not all three of  the coaches who are suspended  provisionally from the major just  peacemaker who is accused of using the  free roam bug the other two are accused  of using the static bug the last one is  called the third person bug according to  esic this bug affected 47 participants  across 97 incidents and quote appeared  to be triggered by a server software  issue on two particular cs go tournament  platforms the third person spectator bug  allowed the affected participant to  observe the game from a locked  third-person view of the team's players  the affected participant was able to  cycle between players altering their  viewpoint accordingly further the  affected participant was able to  manipulate their viewport around the  player's access in a 360 degree range of  motion the bug appeared to resolve  itself at the end of a round and in many  cases coaches simply disconnected  reconnected which resolved the issue  having said that this means that this  bug affected participants for typically  one round or less it affected more  participants than the free roam bug but  was substantially less common than the  static spectator bug which of course had  to be set up intentionally now e6 view  isn't that this bug should be ignored  far from it their official guidance is  that a coach that was experiencing the  bugs should have flagged it admin then  disconnected and reconnected to the  server  under the guidance of an admin it's not  clear exactly how many coaches actually  did this so if you wrote it out to the  end of the round just to see what would  happen  you're facing punishment e6 says that if  you disconnected and thus played less  than a full round under the bug  they are gonna have to handle that on a  case-by-case basis in addition to  clearly setting the competitive  standards for counter-strike in the  future  it's also pretty clear that if coaches  had actually reported this it probably  could have been handled far quicker than  it was cheating might have been  prevented the investigation might have  been shortened so for every round that a  coach played under this bug they're now  facing a 3